<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Union of the Crowns</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1603">1603</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Union of the Crowns was the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> to the thrones of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and Ireland on the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> in March 1603. The kingdoms kept their own parliaments, laws and churches but shared one monarch, who now lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> through the Privy Council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>. The personal union lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1707">Acts of Union</a> of 1707.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In August 1503 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iv-of-scotland">James IV of Scotland</a> married Margaret, eldest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry VII of England</a>, the outcome of the Treaty of Perpetual Peace of the year before. The marriage brought the Scottish Stuarts into the Tudor line of succession, though no Scottish prince was then likely to inherit the English throne.</p><p>The peace did not last. In 1513 James invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-flodden">Battle of Flodden</a>, which England won decisively. In the decades after, England repeatedly invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. By the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>’s reign it was clear to all that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a>, great-grandson of James IV and Margaret, was the only generally acceptable heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From 1601 Elizabeth’s chief minister, Sir Robert Cecil, kept up a secret correspondence with James to prepare a smooth succession. In March 1603, with the queen dying, Cecil sent him a draft proclamation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> was placed under guard. Elizabeth died in the early hours of 24 March, and within eight hours James was proclaimed king in London without protest or disturbance. James left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> on 5 April 1603, promising to return every three years; he came back only once, in 1617. His English coronation took place on 25 July.</p><p>In his first speech to the English Parliament, on 19 March 1604, James likened himself to a husband to the whole island and said he should not be a polygamist with two wives. Parliament feared the loss of the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and legal opinion held that a union would end the established laws of both countries. On 20 October 1604 James took the title King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Ireland by proclamation, which deepened the offence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In October 1604 English and Scottish commissioners were appointed to explore a union. They made limited progress on border laws, trade and citizenship, but free trade and equal rights before the law proved contentious.</p><p>The Scottish Parliament feared the lesser being drawn by the greater, and anti-English satires proliferated. James devised new coats of arms and a uniform coinage, and on 12 April 1606 proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-flag">Union Flag</a>. Scots who saw in it the cross of St George laid over the saltire of St Andrew made a design of their own with the reverse arrangement.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and Ireland stayed separate states with separate parliaments until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1707">Acts of Union</a> of 1707 united England and Scotland into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a>. Ireland kept a legally separate Crown and Parliament, as a practical dependency, until 1801.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Union of the Crowns” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Union of the Crowns</b> in 1603, James VI governed Scotland from London through the Privy Council in Edinburgh, claiming that he ruled it with his pen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
